      "text": "The probability it will rain on Saturday is $60\\%$ and the probability it will rain on Sunday is $25\\%$. Since the probability of rain on a given day is independent of the weather on any other day, we can calculate the probability of rain on both days by multiplying the probabilities of rain on each day.\nThe probability it will rain on both days is $60\\% \\cdot 25\\% = 0.6 \\cdot 0.25 = 0.15$.\nTo express the probability as a percent, we multiply by 100: $0.15 \\cdot 100 = \\boxed{15\\%}$.\n\nTherefore, the probability it will rain on both days is $\\boxed{15\\%}$.The answer is: 15",
