    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to calculate the probability that both Saturday and Sunday will rain. The probability of an independent event occurring is the product of the probabilities of the individual events.\n\nLet's denote the probability that it will rain on Saturday as \\( P(S) \\) and the probability that it will rain on Sunday as \\( P(U) \\). We are given:\n\\[ P(S) = 0.60 \\]\n\\[ P(U) = 0.25 \\]\n\nThe probability that it will rain on both days, \\( P(S \\cap U) \\), is the product of the probabilities of the two independent events:\n\\[ P(S \\cap U) = P(S) \\times P(U) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication:\n\\[ 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15 \\]\n\nTo express this probability as a percent, we multiply by 100:\n\\[ 0.15 \\times 100 = 15\\% \\]\n\nThus, the probability that it will rain on both Saturday and Sunday is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
